"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdocs=self.webpackChunkdocs||[]).push([["6607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"rev-dep-3-3-release","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-3-release","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-14-rev-dep-3-3-release/index.mdx","title":"Rev-dep 3.3.0 is here!","description":"This version brings baseline snapshots for config checks.","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":0.76,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Jakub Mazurek","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://jayu.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jayu"},"socials":{"x":"https://x.com/jayu_dev","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakub-mazurek-57065a171/","github":"https://github.com/jayu"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/jayu.png","key":"jayu"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"rev-dep-3-3-release","title":"Rev-dep 3.3.0 is here!","authors":["jayu"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.2.1 release","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-2-1-release"}},"content":"This version brings baseline snapshots for config checks.\\n\\nAdding checks to a large codebase can uncover more issues than you can fix right away. You can now acknowledge existing findings and keep CI passing while you work through them gradually.\\n\\nAdd `baselineSnapshotPath` to your config, and record the current findings:\\n\\n```bash\\nrev-dep config run --update-snapshot\\n```\\n\\nCommit the snapshot alongside your config. CI keeps running `rev-dep config run` as usual. New findings fail the run, and fixed issues become obsolete findings that also require a snapshot update. \\n\\nFindings are saved in formatting-agnostic way so moving an import (eg. of missing dependency) to another line doesn\'t trigger new violations to the baseline.\\n\\nDuplicated code keeps its existing snapshot mechanism; the same update flag handles both.\\n\\nRead more in the [findings baseline docs](/docs/config-based-checks/findings-baseline).\\n\\n<br/>\\n\\nThat\'s it for this update, see you in the next one \u{1F44B}"},{"id":"rev-dep-3-2-1-release","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-2-1-release","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-10-rev-dep-3-2-1-release/index.mdx","title":"Rev-dep 3.2.1 release","description":"This patch version bump brings:","date":"2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":4.39,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Jakub Mazurek","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://jayu.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jayu"},"socials":{"x":"https://x.com/jayu_dev","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakub-mazurek-57065a171/","github":"https://github.com/jayu"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/jayu.png","key":"jayu"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"rev-dep-3-2-1-release","title":"Rev-dep 3.2.1 release","authors":["jayu"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.3.0 is here!","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-3-release"},"nextItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.2.0 release","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-2-release"}},"content":"This patch version bump brings:\\n\\n- a fix for the npm wrapper truncating output and exiting with code `0` on failure\\n- validation of `config run` flag values\\n- `--lint-config` support in the `json` and `issues-list` output formats\\n- the same `--fix` exit code in every output format\\n- better detection of test files in `config init`\\n- a fix for the `--follow-monorepo-packages` syntax in the docs and hints\\n\\n[GitHub Release](https://github.com/jayu/rev-dep/releases/tag/3.2.1)\\n\\nThe first three fixes can make a CI run that passed before fail after the upgrade. In each case the run should have been failing all along - the problem was hidden. Please read these sections before upgrading.\\n\\n## npm wrapper output and exit code\\n\\nWhen you run rev-dep installed from npm, a small Node.js script starts the native binary for your platform. Previously, that script collected the whole output in memory before printing it, which caused three problems:\\n\\n- output larger than 1 MiB (Node.js implicit default) was cut off, and the process exited with code `0` even when checks failed\\n- when the output was piped, for example to `tee`, `jq` or a CI log processor, a failing run was cut off after 64 KB\\n- arguments containing `\\"`, `$` or backticks were interpreted by a shell instead of being passed to the binary\\n\\nOn a large repository, this meant `config run` could fail and CI would still pass, and `--format json` could produce a truncated, invalid document.\\n\\nNow the binary writes directly to the terminal, pipe or file, so the output is never cut off and the exit code is always the binary\'s own. Arguments are passed to the binary exactly as given.\\n\\nIf your CI started failing after the upgrade, check the output - those failures were there before, the wrapper just hid them.\\n\\n## Validation of `config run` flags\\n\\nSome `config run` flag values were silently ignored. Now they fail with an error before any check runs:\\n\\n- `--format` accepts only `json` and `issues-list`. Previously, any other value, including a different case like `JSON`, fell back to the default output.\\n- `--workspaces` fails when any listed path doesn\'t match a workspace in the config. Previously, a typo was only reported when none of the paths matched, so `--workspaces packages/app,packages/tpyo` silently ran only `packages/app`. Paths must be written exactly as in the config file.\\n- `--lint-config-rules` is validated before the run. Previously, an unknown rule name was reported only after all checks had finished.\\n\\n## Config lint in machine-readable output\\n\\nPreviously, `--lint-config` and `--lint-config-rules` were silently skipped when used with `--format json` or `--format issues-list`. A CI step combining them passed even when the config lint found errors.\\n\\nNow the config is linted in every output format, and lint errors fail the run. With `--format issues-list`, the same summary line as in the default output is printed after the issues. With `--format json`, the counts are reported in a new top-level `lint` field:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"version\\": \\"2.2\\",\\n  \\"hasFailures\\": false,\\n  \\"workspaces\\": [...],\\n  \\"fixSummary\\": {...},\\n  \\"lint\\": { \\"errors\\": 1, \\"warnings\\": 0 }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe field is present only when the config was linted. Lint findings don\'t set `hasFailures`, which still reports check failures only. The JSON output version is now `2.2` - validate against the [2.2 schema](https://github.com/jayu/rev-dep/blob/master/output-schema/2.2.schema.json). Read more about [linting during a config run](/docs/config-based-checks/linting-the-config#linting-during-a-config-run).\\n\\n## The same `--fix` exit code in every format\\n\\nWith `--fix`, the default output exits with code `0` when every issue found was fixable and has been fixed. The `json` and `issues-list` formats exited with code `1` in that case, unless `--recheck` was also used.\\n\\nNow all formats follow the same rule: `0` when everything found was fixed, `1` when an issue remains that `--fix` can\'t fix. In JSON output, `hasFailures` still reports that the checks found issues before they were fixed. Read more about [exit codes with `--fix` and `--recheck`](/docs/config-based-checks/running-checks-and-autofix#exit-code-nuance-with---fix-and---recheck).\\n\\n## Test files detection in `config init`\\n\\n`config init` sorts the detected entry points into `prodEntryPoints` and `devEntryPoints`. Some common test file names were not recognized as development files, so they ended up in `prodEntryPoints`:\\n\\n- `*.e2e-spec.ts`, used by NestJS\\n- `*.e2e-test.ts`, `*.integration-test.ts` and `*.integration-spec.ts`\\n- Jest config and setup files like `jest-e2e.ts` or `jest.setup.ts`\\n\\nA generated config then treated test code as production code - in one large project, 100 test files ended up as production entry points. Now these files are classified as development entry points. This affects only newly generated configs.\\n\\n## `--follow-monorepo-packages` syntax\\n\\nTo follow only selected packages, the list has to be passed with `=`:\\n\\n```bash\\nrev-dep resolve --file packages/app/src/main.tsx --follow-monorepo-packages=@acme/shared,@acme/ui\\n```\\n\\nWritten with a space, the list is not read as the flag\'s value, and every workspace package is followed. The docs and the hint printed after a restricted-imports failure showed the form with a space. Both are fixed, and the flag\'s help text now shows the `=` form. Read more about [following monorepo packages](/docs/other-concepts-and-features/following-monorepo-packages).\\n\\n<br/>\\n\\nThat\'s it for this update. See you in the next one \u{1F44B}"},{"id":"rev-dep-3-2-release","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-2-release","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-04-rev-dep-3-2-release/index.mdx","title":"Rev-dep 3.2.0 release","description":"This minor version bump brings:","date":"2026-10-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.13,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Jakub Mazurek","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://jayu.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jayu"},"socials":{"x":"https://x.com/jayu_dev","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakub-mazurek-57065a171/","github":"https://github.com/jayu"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/jayu.png","key":"jayu"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"rev-dep-3-2-release","title":"Rev-dep 3.2.0 release","authors":["jayu"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.2.1 release","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-2-1-release"},"nextItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.1.0 release","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-1-release"}},"content":"This minor version bump brings:\\n\\n- a fix for `.gitignore` lookup in git worktrees and submodules\\n- a new `failOnEmptyWorkspace` config option\\n- improvements to telemetry reporting\\n\\n[GitHub Release](https://github.com/jayu/rev-dep/releases/tag/3.2.0)\\n\\nThis release exists thanks to a community contribution. Big thanks for reporting [issue #46](https://github.com/jayu/rev-dep/issues/46) - it came with a minimal reproduction, the root cause, and a suggestion for how to prevent this kind of problem in the future. Both the fix and the suggestion are part of this release.\\n\\n## Gitignore lookup in worktrees and submodules\\n\\nRev-dep reads `.gitignore` files from parent folders, up to the repository root. Previously, it recognized the root only by a `.git` directory. Linked git worktrees and submodules use a `.git` file instead, so the lookup continued into the enclosing checkout and applied its ignore rules.\\n\\nWhen a worktree was nested in a folder ignored by the main checkout - for example `.agent/worktrees/`, where an AI agent creates its worktrees - every source file was excluded. `config run` reported zero files and still passed.\\n\\nNow the lookup stops at a `.git` file the same way it stops at a `.git` directory, which is how git itself treats repository boundaries.\\n\\n## Fail on empty workspace\\n\\nThe issue also pointed out a bigger problem: a check that runs on zero files proves nothing, but it still passes. That is easy to miss in CI or in AI agent runs, where nobody reads the warnings.\\n\\nThe new `failOnEmptyWorkspace` option makes such a workspace fail the run:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"configVersion\\": \\"2.1\\",\\n  \\"failOnEmptyWorkspace\\": true,\\n  \\"workspaces\\": [\\n    { \\"path\\": \\"packages/app\\" },\\n    { \\"path\\": \\"packages/placeholder\\", \\"failOnEmptyWorkspace\\": false }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe option is set at the config root and can be overridden per workspace. It is opt-in, so existing configs behave the same unless you enable it. \\n\\nNew configs generated by `rev-dep config init` enable it by default.\\n\\nWithout the option, an empty workspace is still reported as a warning - and it is now also listed in the summary at the end of `config run`, so it doesn\'t get lost in the output.\\n\\nThe option requires config version `2.1`. Read more in the [config file structure docs](/docs/config-based-checks/config-file-structure#top-level-fields).\\n\\n## Telemetry improvements\\n\\nThe anonymized repository identifier is now resolved correctly in worktrees and submodules, and it falls back to the `repository` field from `package.json` when no git remote is available. Events also report where the identifier came from. Read more about [telemetry](/docs/telemetry).\\n\\n<br/>\\n\\nThat\'s it for this update. See you in the next one \u{1F44B}"},{"id":"rev-dep-3-1-release","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-1-release","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-29-rev-dep-3-1-release/index.mdx","title":"Rev-dep 3.1.0 release","description":"This minor version bump brings:","date":"2026-09-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":1.47,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Jakub Mazurek","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://jayu.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jayu"},"socials":{"x":"https://x.com/jayu_dev","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakub-mazurek-57065a171/","github":"https://github.com/jayu"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/jayu.png","key":"jayu"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"rev-dep-3-1-release","title":"Rev-dep 3.1.0 release","authors":["jayu"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.2.0 release","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-2-release"},"nextItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.0 is here!","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-0-release"}},"content":"This minor version bump brings:\\n\\n- parsing template string literals inside require and import calls\\n- ability to detect non-literal import targets\\n- improved support for built-in modules detection\\n- improvements to telemetry reporting\\n- various documentation improvements\\n\\n[GitHub Release](https://github.com/jayu/rev-dep/releases/tag/3.1.0)\\n\\n## Template literal import targets\\n\\nPreviously, imports with template string literals, for example ``import(`someModule.ts`)``, were not parsed. Right now they are, as long as the target value can be statically analysed.\\n\\nTemplate literals that cannot be statically analysed, for example ``import(`${dir}/someModule.ts`)``, will be internally represented as `NonLiteralModule`. Such imports cannot be followed, but they can be detected as a violation by the `unresolvedImports` detector.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"unresolvedImportsDetection\\": {\\n    \\"enabled\\": true,\\n    \\"reportNonLiteralImports\\": true\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe detector will throw when there is any non-literal import in the analysed workspace.\\n\\n## Improved built-in modules support\\n\\nPreviously, Rev-dep recognized built-in modules like `fs` or `bun:sqlite`, but it was not possible to make assertions against them.\\n\\nNow you can refer to them by name in the following detectors:\\n- [Restricted Importers](/docs/config-based-checks/checks/restricted-importers)\\n- [Restricted Direct Importers](/docs/config-based-checks/checks/restricted-direct-importers)\\n- [Restricted Imports](/docs/config-based-checks/checks/restricted-imports)\\n\\nYou can also refer to any built-in module with the `builtin:*` keyword - useful for restricting imports in front-end code.\\n\\nRead more about [built-in modules support in docs](/docs/other-concepts-and-features/built-in-modules)\\n\\n## Telemetry improvements\\n\\nAdded event reporting to exploratory toolkit commands. Added reporting of an anonymized repository identifier to all events.\\n\\n## Docs improvements\\n\\nAdded Newsletter and Blog pages. Added llms.txt to improve discoverability by AI agents. Improved the integration guides and the comparison with other tools page.\\n\\n<br/>\\n\\nThat\'s it for this update. See you in the next one \u{1F44B}"},{"id":"rev-dep-3-0-release","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-0-release","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-03-rev-dep-3-0-release/index.mdx","title":"Rev-dep 3.0 is here!","description":"This version brings performance improvements, duplicate code detection, and glob matcher behavior parity with gitignore.","date":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":5.79,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Jakub Mazurek","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://jayu.dev","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/jayu"},"socials":{"x":"https://x.com/jayu_dev","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakub-mazurek-57065a171/","github":"https://github.com/jayu"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/jayu.png","key":"jayu"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"rev-dep-3-0-release","title":"Rev-dep 3.0 is here!","authors":["jayu"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Rev-dep 3.1.0 release","permalink":"/blog/rev-dep-3-1-release"}},"content":"This version brings performance improvements, duplicate code detection, and glob matcher behavior parity with gitignore.\\n\\nMake sure to check [Short Story of Rev-dep](#short-story-of-rev-dep) at the end of this post!\\n\\nUseful links:\\n- [Breaking changes and migration guide](/docs/upgrade-guides/v3-breaking-changes)<br/>\\n- [Github Release](https://github.com/jayu/Rev-dep/releases/tag/3.0.0)\\n\\n## Performance Improvements\\n\\nOn the codebase I use for benchmarking, which has around 600k LoC, **Rev-dep config run** was executing for ~300ms prior to the performance improvements; now it executes for ~150ms.\\n\\nIt got 50% faster and now there is likely no more room for improvements.\\n\\nThe gap was mostly caused by inefficient data-structure locks, which effectively made the resolution phase single-threaded. There were also some improvements in the parser.\\n\\nThis was also delivered to version **2.x** in release **2.9.1**.\\n\\n## Duplicate Code Detection\\n\\nDuplicate code detection is a new feature that I was excited to build. I\'ve tried to do it a few years ago in my other tool [CodeQue](https://codeque.co), but due to JavaScript threading limitations, it was not feasible to implement it in a performant way.\\n\\nI took lessons from that approach and implemented it within Rev-dep, utilizing the full ownership of the parser and Go\'s concurrency model. The result is that you can add detection on top of existing Rev-dep checks without sacrificing performance.\\n\\nWhat\'s different in Rev-dep\'s duplicate code identification approach is that it focuses on code chunks that can be refactored right away. It captures duplicated code blocks and JSX expressions, while other tools focus on detecting chunks of duplicated tokens. But those tokens can start in the middle of some function and end in the middle of another, which makes the findings harder to work with.\\n\\nRead more about duplicate code detection in [duplicated code detection docs](/docs/other-concepts-and-features/duplicated-code-detection).\\n\\n## Glob Matchers Behavior Parity With Gitignore\\n\\nAnyone who has built a static analysis tool knows how tricky it is to implement glob matchers that behave the same way as gitignore. The 2.x approach had some gaps in the implementation, and they couldn\'t be fixed without breaking changes.\\n\\nI believe choosing to be compliant with gitignore is a good choice because you have to support it anyway. Otherwise, projects might need to adjust their gitignore files, and it has become an industry standard due to Git\'s adoption.\\n\\nAll glob matchers in Rev-dep now follow gitignore behavior. Matching is still very fast thanks to the [gobwas/glob](https://github.com/gobwas/glob) library, which can parse glob expressions into data structures that enable very efficient matching.\\n\\n## Short Story of Rev-dep\\n\\nThis release is a great opportunity to look back at the origin of Rev-dep. \\n\\nIt started as a CLI with just the **rev-dep resolve** command, serving the purpose of finding where a given React component was used in a server-side React application.\\n\\nBack then I was working on a project where we migrated a CSS solution in a multi-page React product, and we needed a way to shortlist all pages affected by a given batch of changes.\\n\\nThe initial release happened in November 2020.\\n\\nThe **0.x** version was working, but was not robust enough to work on very large codebases with complex dependency graphs.\\n\\nIt was based on [dependency-cruiser](/docs/comparison-with-other-tools/dependency-cruiser-vs-rev-dep), which delivered the dependency graph. Later it also used [dpdm](/docs/comparison-with-other-tools/dpdm-vs-rev-dep), which added support for TS codebases (yes, at that time people still worked on JS-only codebases \u{1F600}).\\n\\nNow Rev-dep aims to replace these tools.\\n\\n### 1.x Version (2022-2025)\\n\\nVersion 1.0 included a performance improvement to the poorly designed internal graph structure that was actually a tree with a lot of duplicate nodes instead of a graph that connects unique nodes.\\nBefore the fix, the tool was allocating a lot of memory and was not able to finish a task on more complex codebases.\\n\\nThis release also brought more commands, such as getting project entry points and listing files reachable from a given entry point.\\n\\nAs part of Rev-dep, I\'ve also written a Babel-based codemod, purpose-built to refactor imports from barrel files (*export * from \'./x\'*) into direct imports.\\nThat was built to solve dev server performance issues in another project I was working on.\\n\\nAt that time, Rev-dep still had a small scope and the CLI tool was not fast enough to use conveniently.\\n\\n### 2.x Version - Revamp in Go (2025-2026)\\n\\nIn 2025, Microsoft announced that they were porting the TypeScript compiler to Go and had achieved around a 10x performance improvement.\\n\\nAt that time, I was learning Go and built a side project with it. The TypeScript story inspired me to try to port Rev-dep to Go and see if I could achieve similar performance improvements.\\n\\nInitially, I thought that I would be able to use their brand new TypeScript parser written in Go, but it turned out they hadn\'t exposed a public API for it yet. In fact, even now with the TS 7.0 release, there is no public parser API.\\nInstead of forking their WIP parser, I looked for some alternatives and reminded myself about [ESbuild](https://esbuild.github.io/), which is a fast JS/TS bundler written in Go. \\nI found a way to get the dependency graph from esbuild compilation, and I implemented a prototype of **Rev-dep&nbsp;resolve** based on it. It was about 10 times faster than the previous JS version, and I was impressed.\\n\\nBuilding on top of ESbuild was not feasible for all features, as it only exposes runtime dependencies - all type-only imports have been dropped. Ultimately, it is a bundler, and I used bundling metadata for my PoC.\\nBut I already knew that reimplementing Rev-dep in Go would make it much faster and pleasant to use day-to-day.\\nWith the help of Gen AI, I was able to implement a parser from scratch. It was the best possible parser for the job - it was purely focused on extracting imports from source code files and did not even build the AST. After the resolver implementation and parallelization of the processing, the end result was a CLI tool that was 40 times faster than the previous JS version.\\n\\nFast forward to today, Rev-dep 3.0 is now a fully featured tool that can be used to analyze and enforce dependency hygiene in large codebases. It has a rich set of checks, including circular dependencies, orphan files, unused exports, and more. The tool is also highly configurable, allowing users to define their own rules and checks.\\nEven with Gen AI, it took a lot of time and effort to get there, but I\'m really happy with the result.\\n\\n<br/>\\n\\nThat\'s it for this update, see you in the next one \u{1F44B}"}]}}')}}]);